\setlength{\footskip}{8mm}

\chapter{RESULT}

This chapter reports the results of the four-condition, four-backbone experiment. Evaluation proceeds in three layers. \textbf{Layer~1 (absolute grading):} a blind GPT-4o judge scores each of the 1,600 complete sessions (4 backbones $\times$ 4 conditions $\times$ 100 dialogues) on seven metrics. \textbf{Layer~2 (paired statistics):} per-dialogue paired deltas and $t$-tests between conditions sharing the same problem seed. \textbf{Layer~3 (pairwise preference):} 800 forced-choice comparisons (Dissonance-Aware vs.\ Baseline and vs.\ Multimodal, per backbone, per seed) under the two-order swap-consistency protocol of Zheng et al.\ \cite{zheng2023judging}, with a cross-family second judge (Claude Sonnet 4.6) providing inter-judge agreement on a stratified 100-comparison subset. All dialogue files were verified complete before evaluation (100/100 dialogues in each of the 16 backbone--condition cells; 10 sequential turns with all required fields).

The four therapist configurations are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Baseline:} Text-only prompting without any emotional metadata.
    \item \textbf{Emotion (Text-Only):} The therapist receives the client's text along with text-derived Valence-Arousal (VA) values.
    \item \textbf{Multimodal (Vocal-Aware):} The therapist receives both text-derived VA and speech-derived VA from the client's vocal cues plus prosodic descriptors, but without explicit dissonance computation.
    \item \textbf{Dissonance-Aware:} The therapist receives text VA, speech VA, and the computed delta between modalities (emotional dissonance signal), enabling the model to detect and respond to mismatches between what the client says and how they sound.
\end{itemize}

GPT-4o scored each full dialogue session on seven quantitative metrics spanning three evaluation dimensions: \textbf{Therapist Skills} (Understanding, Interpersonal Effectiveness), \textbf{Client Alliance} (Affective Bond), and \textbf{CTRS} items (Collaboration, Guided Discovery, Focus, Strategy) \cite{young1980ctrs,kim-etal-2025-mirror}.

\textbf{Key findings.} First, on GPT-4o mini the Dissonance-Aware condition achieved the highest score on \emph{all seven} metrics, with statistically significant gains over Baseline on the three CTRS dimensions that measure session craft: Guided Discovery ($+0.24$, $t = 3.1$), Focus ($+0.34$, $t = 4.7$), and Strategy ($+0.17$, $t = 2.7$); the pairwise layer confirms the advantage (net win-rate $0.680$, sign-test $p = 0.0001$). Second, the Multimodal (Vocal-Aware) condition \emph{regressed below Baseline} on both weaker backbones (GPT-4o mini: total-score $t = -4.75$; Qwen: $t = -6.13$), while Dissonance-Aware defeated Multimodal decisively wherever the judge could discriminate (GPT-4o mini: $+0.75$ on Guided Discovery, $t = 10.1$; pairwise net win-rate $0.800$, $p < 0.00001$). The practical reading is the central mechanistic claim of this thesis: \emph{raw vocal metadata is not intrinsically beneficial; the dissonance framing is what converts vocal signals into therapeutic gain}. Third, Emotion (Text-Only) was statistically indistinguishable from Baseline on GPT-4o mini ($t = -0.03$) and significantly worse on Qwen ($t = -3.19$): appending VA numbers alone does not help. Fourth, near-ceiling backbones (Claude, DeepSeek) are only partially measurable on the absolute scale; the pairwise layer nevertheless recovers a significant dissonance advantage on DeepSeek ($p = 0.00004$) that absolute scores completely hide.

\section{Result Corresponding to RQ1: Dissonance Advantage}

RQ1 asks whether the Dissonance-Aware therapist framework, which computes emotional mismatch (VA delta) between text and speech, outperforms the three comparison conditions. Table~\ref{tab:evaluation_results} presents the Layer-1 average scores across 100 dialogues on GPT-4o mini.

Dissonance-Aware attained the highest mean on every metric. Over Baseline, the gains concentrate on the CTRS craft dimensions: Guided Discovery rose from 4.10 to 4.34 ($+0.24$), Focus from 4.11 to 4.45 ($+0.34$), and Strategy from 3.99 to 4.16 ($+0.17$), with Understanding and Collaboration improving by $+0.06$ and $+0.05$. Over Multimodal (Vocal-Aware), the advantage is markedly larger: Guided Discovery $+0.75$ (3.59 $\rightarrow$ 4.34), Strategy $+0.62$, Focus $+0.52$, and Interpersonal Effectiveness $+0.29$. The Multimodal condition itself fell \emph{below} Baseline on six of seven metrics (largest: Guided Discovery $-0.51$, Strategy $-0.45$), and Emotion (Text-Only) tracked Baseline within $\pm 0.08$ on all metrics.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\caption{Average evaluation scores across 100 dialogues comparing 4 therapist generation methods (GPT-4o mini backbone, blind GPT-4o judge, Layer~1)}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\hline
\multicolumn{1}{c}{\textbf{Method}} & \textbf{Under.} & \textbf{Interp.} & \textbf{Affect.} & \textbf{CTRS-C} & \textbf{CTRS-G} & \textbf{CTRS-F} & \textbf{CTRS-S} & \textbf{Succ.} \\ \hline
Baseline                            & 4.92 & 4.91 & 3.93 & 4.91 & 4.10 & 4.11 & 3.99 & 100/100 \\
Emotion (Text-Only)                 & 4.93 & 4.83 & 3.89 & 4.85 & 4.12 & 4.26 & 3.98 & 100/100 \\
Multimodal (Vocal-Aware)            & 4.94 & 4.68 & 3.84 & 4.57 & 3.59 & 3.93 & 3.54 & 100/100 \\
\textbf{Dissonance-Aware}           & \textbf{4.98} & \textbf{4.97} & \textbf{3.98} & \textbf{4.96} & \textbf{4.34} & \textbf{4.45} & \textbf{4.16} & 100/100 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}%
}
\label{tab:evaluation_results}
\end{table}

These findings support H1 on this backbone with respect to all three comparison conditions: Dissonance-Aware is the highest-scoring condition on every metric, significantly so on the CTRS craft dimensions against Baseline and on every discriminative dimension against Multimodal.

\subsection{Statistical Significance and the Role of the Dissonance Signal}
\label{subsec:significance}

Table~\ref{tab:significance} reports Layer-2 paired $t$-tests over the 100 per-dialogue total scores (sum of the seven metrics) for GPT-4o mini. The information ladder of the v1-era analysis has inverted. Adding text VA (Emotion) no longer improves over Baseline ($t = -0.03$). Adding vocal metadata without dissonance framing (Multimodal) now \emph{degrades} quality significantly ($t = -4.75$ vs.\ Baseline; $t = -4.89$ vs.\ Emotion). Adding the dissonance computation on top of the identical vocal inputs (Dissonance-Aware) reverses the sign entirely: $+2.75$ total points over Multimodal ($t = 7.94$) and $+0.97$ over Baseline ($t = 3.53$). Per-metric tests sharpen the picture: Dissonance-Aware differs from Baseline significantly on Guided Discovery ($t = 3.1$), Focus ($t = 4.7$), and Strategy ($t = 2.7$), and differs from Multimodal on all six non-Understanding metrics ($t \geq 3.5$, largest on Guided Discovery, $t = 10.1$). Composite win/tie/loss counts agree: 52/29/19 against Baseline and 73/21/6 against Multimodal.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\centering
\caption{Paired $t$-tests on per-dialogue total scores (GPT-4o mini, 100 dialogues, Layer~2)}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
\textbf{Comparison} & \textbf{Mean $\Delta$} & \textbf{$t$} & \textbf{Interpretation} \\ \hline
Dissonance $-$ Baseline   & +0.97 & +3.53 & significant \\
Dissonance $-$ Multimodal & +2.75 & +7.94 & significant \\
Dissonance $-$ Emotion    & +0.98 & +3.40 & significant \\
Multimodal $-$ Baseline   & $-$1.78 & $-$4.75 & significant (regression) \\
Emotion $-$ Baseline      & $-$0.01 & $-$0.03 & not significant \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:significance}
\end{table}

\subsection{Dissonance Flag Rate and the Always-On Framing Effect}

The binary dissonance flag ($|\delta_V| \geq 0.5$ or $|\delta_A| \geq 0.5$) remains rare under synthetic speech: it fired on 0.8\% of GPT-4o mini turns, 1.2\% of Qwen turns, 0.9\% of DeepSeek turns, and 3.7\% of Claude turns (Table~\ref{tab:flagrate-backbone}). Because the Zonos director assigns vocal emotion from the text content, speech VA tracks text VA closely and large natural mismatch is uncommon. Yet the Dissonance$-$Multimodal gap is large and significant precisely where backbones have scoring headroom (GPT-4o mini: $+2.75$, $t = 7.94$; Qwen: $+0.60$, $t = 2.53$). Since the flag fires on $\sim$1\% of turns, the gap cannot be driven by flagged turns alone. The mechanism is the \emph{always-on framing}: the Dissonance-Aware prompt supplies the continuous deltas $\delta_V, \delta_A$ on every turn together with interpretive guidance (``large $|$delta$|$ means tone and words are pulling in different directions; explore gently''), which changes how the therapist weighs vocal information on \emph{every} turn, not only flagged ones.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\centering
\caption{Dissonance flag rate and Dissonance$-$Multimodal paired $t$-test on total scores by backbone (100 dialogues each)}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
\textbf{Backbone} & \textbf{Flag rate} & \textbf{Mean $\Delta$ (total)} & \textbf{$t$} \\ \hline
GPT-4o mini       & 0.8\% & +2.75 & +7.94 \\
Qwen-2.5-72B      & 1.2\% & +0.60 & +2.53 \\
DeepSeek V3.2     & 0.9\% & +0.03 & +0.46 \\
Claude Sonnet 4.6 & 3.7\% & $-$1.05 & $-$3.47 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:flagrate-backbone}
\end{table}

Two complementary observations complete the mechanistic picture. First, without the framing, the same vocal descriptors actively hurt: Multimodal regressed below Baseline on GPT-4o mini (Guided Discovery $-0.51$, $t = -7.1$) and Qwen ($-0.44$, $t = -7.9$), and qualitative inspection of the judge's reasoning on the weakest Multimodal sessions describes repetitive, surface-level responses that reference tone without deepening exploration. Second, the flag rate does not track the size of the Dissonance$-$Multimodal gap (Claude has the highest flag rate but sits at the scoring ceiling; GPT-4o mini has the lowest but the largest gap), reinforcing that headroom and framing, not flag frequency, drive the effect. The scripted real-voice demonstration (Section~\ref{sec:chat-demo}) shows the flagged case working as designed: with a genuine mismatch ($\delta_V = -1.05$), the therapist responds with targeted, non-accusatory exploration.

\section{Result Corresponding to RQ2: Backbone Robustness}

RQ2 asks whether the Dissonance-Aware framework's advantage persists when the underlying therapist LLM backbone is changed. The full 4-condition experiment was replicated across GPT-4o mini, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct, and DeepSeek V3.2 on the same 100 shared problem seeds, yielding 1,600 Layer-1 sessions, 800 Layer-3 comparisons ($\times 2$ presentation orders), and 200 second-judge calls.

\subsection{Cross-Backbone Comparison: Dissonance-Aware Only}

Table~\ref{tab:cross-backbone-dissonance} compares all four backbones under the Dissonance-Aware condition. Claude Sonnet 4.6 leads across all metrics, followed by DeepSeek V3.2, with GPT-4o mini and Qwen-2.5-72B trailing. The ordering matches the general capability ranking of the models: the dissonance signal does not wash out intrinsic model quality but adds a complementary benefit whose measurable size depends on each backbone's headroom below the judge's ceiling.

