Table~\ref{tab:claude-results} shows the 4-condition results for Claude Sonnet 4.6. Every condition scores above 5.0 on all six 0--6 metrics: the backbone sits at the judge's ceiling. On absolute scores Multimodal is nominally highest (5.24) and Dissonance$-$Baseline is not significant ($t = -1.01$ on totals). The Layer-3 evidence indicates these nominal orderings are not measurable differences: the pairwise judge declared 99--100\% ties with near-zero position consistency (0.00--0.01), meaning its verdicts flip when presentation order is swapped, i.e.\ the sessions are perceptually equivalent to the judge (Section~\ref{sec:pairwise-results}). The cross-family second judge, which does commit to winners on the same transcripts, preferred Dissonance over Multimodal 10-to-2 on the sampled subset. We therefore read Claude as \emph{inconclusive at ceiling} rather than as a refutation: consistent with MIRROR's observation that strong backbones leave little headroom for emotional metadata to act \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror}.

