\label{tab:dissonance-uplift}
\end{table}

The uplift is significant on two of four backbones (GPT-4o mini and DeepSeek V3.2), neutral on Qwen, and unmeasurable on Claude, where all conditions saturate the scale. Unlike the monotonic inverse-headroom pattern of the earlier stateless-client pilot, the v4 pattern is \emph{headroom-gated}: the advantage appears where the judge can still discriminate, and the discriminative power --- not the raw capability of the backbone --- is the binding constraint. The most robust cross-backbone regularity is not Dissonance vs.\ Baseline but Dissonance vs.\ Multimodal: positive on three of four backbones (significantly so on GPT-4o mini and Qwen) and never significantly negative.

\section{Pairwise Preference Results}
\label{sec:pairwise-results}

Because the absolute scale saturates for strong backbones (Sections 5.2.4--5.2.6), the primary discriminative instrument is the pairwise layer. Each comparison presents both complete sessions to the blind GPT-4o judge in a single prompt with a forced choice (A better / B better / tie). Following the conservative protocol of Zheng et al.\ \cite{zheng2023judging}, every comparison is judged twice with the presentation order swapped; a win is awarded only if the same session wins in both orders, otherwise the comparison counts as a tie. Interpretation criteria were fixed before the run: $p < 0.05$ with more wins than losses supports dissonance superiority; near-equal counts indicate no detectable difference; $p < 0.05$ with more losses would be evidence against. Significance is an exact two-sided sign test on wins vs.\ losses. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports all 800 comparisons.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\caption{Pairwise preference results (win = Dissonance-Aware; two-order swap consistency; blind GPT-4o judge)}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llccccc cc}
\hline
\textbf{Backbone} & \textbf{Opponent} & \textbf{W} & \textbf{T} & \textbf{L} & \textbf{Net} & \textbf{$p$ (sign)} & \textbf{Pos.\ cons.} & \textbf{$r$(Dlen,out)} \\ \hline
GPT-4o mini   & Baseline   & \textbf{61} & 14 & 25 & \textbf{0.680} & \textbf{0.0001}  & 0.86 & $-$0.17 \\
GPT-4o mini   & Multimodal & \textbf{73} & 14 & 13 & \textbf{0.800} & $\mathbf{< 10^{-4}}$ & 0.86 & 0.00 \\
DeepSeek V3.2 & Baseline   & \textbf{46} & 40 & 14 & \textbf{0.660} & \textbf{0.00004} & 0.60 & +0.06 \\
DeepSeek V3.2 & Multimodal & 25 & 54 & 21 & 0.520 & 0.659 & 0.46 & +0.12 \\
Qwen-2.5-72B  & Baseline   & 36 & 23 & 41 & 0.475 & 0.649 & 0.77 & +0.07 \\
Qwen-2.5-72B  & Multimodal & 45 & 21 & 34 & 0.555 & 0.260 & 0.79 & $-$0.07 \\
Claude S.\ 4.6 & Baseline   & 0 & 99 & 1 & 0.495 & 1.000 & 0.01 & $-$0.01 \\
Claude S.\ 4.6 & Multimodal & 0 & 100 & 0 & 0.500 & 1.000 & 0.00 & n/a \\ \hline
\end{tabular}%
}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

Three patterns stand out. (1) \textbf{Significant wins where headroom exists:} Dissonance-Aware defeats Baseline on GPT-4o mini (net $0.680$, $p = 0.0001$) and DeepSeek (net $0.660$, $p = 0.00004$), and defeats Multimodal overwhelmingly on GPT-4o mini (net $0.800$, $p < 10^{-4}$). (2) \textbf{No significant losses anywhere:} the only cells with more losses than wins (Qwen vs.\ Baseline, 36/41) are statistically indistinguishable from coin flips ($p = 0.649$). (3) \textbf{Ceiling behaves as expected:} on Claude the judge returns ties in 99--100\% of comparisons with position consistency near zero --- its verdicts reverse under order swap, which under the conservative rule means the sessions are not discriminable by this instrument.

\subsection{Bias Controls}

\textbf{Position bias.} Position consistency (the fraction of comparisons where both presentation orders agree \cite{shi2025judging}) is high exactly where real quality differences exist (GPT-4o mini 0.86; Qwen 0.77--0.79) and collapses to $\approx 0$ on Claude, which is itself diagnostic of indistinguishability rather than of judge instability on separable content.

\textbf{Verbosity/length bias.} LLM judges are known to favor longer answers \cite{zheng2023judging}, and MIRROR documents length--score correlations up to $r = 0.6$ for GPT evaluators in therapy scoring \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror}. The judge prompt explicitly forbade rewarding length, and the data confirm no confound: correlations between transcript-length difference and outcome are negligible in every cell ($|r| \leq 0.17$). The strongest result in the study (GPT-4o mini vs.\ Multimodal, net $0.800$) occurs while Dissonance-Aware transcripts are on average \emph{1,310 characters shorter} --- the opposite of a verbosity artifact.

\section{Judge Reliability: Cross-Family Agreement}

To quantify judge reliability beyond self-consistency, a stratified subset of 100 comparisons (proportional allocation across the eight backbone--pair cells, random seed 42) was re-judged under the identical protocol by a second judge from a different model family (Claude Sonnet 4.6, via OpenRouter), controlling for self-enhancement bias \cite{zheng2023judging}. Table~\ref{tab:kappa} reports Cohen's $\kappa$ and raw agreement on the final (post-swap) labels.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\centering
\caption{Inter-judge agreement on the stratified 100-comparison subset (judge1 = GPT-4o, judge2 = Claude Sonnet 4.6)}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\hline
\textbf{Backbone} & \textbf{Opponent} & \textbf{$n$} & \textbf{$\kappa$} & \textbf{Raw agree.} \\ \hline
Qwen-2.5-72B  & Baseline   & 12 & 0.600 & 75.0\% \\
DeepSeek V3.2 & Baseline   & 13 & 0.500 & 69.2\% \\
DeepSeek V3.2 & Multimodal & 13 & 0.444 & 61.5\% \\
GPT-4o mini   & Multimodal & 12 & 0.348 & 58.3\% \\
Qwen-2.5-72B  & Multimodal & 12 & 0.258 & 50.0\% \\
GPT-4o mini   & Baseline   & 12 & 0.158 & 33.3\% \\
Claude S.\ 4.6 & Baseline   & 13 & 0.000 & 30.8\% \\
Claude S.\ 4.6 & Multimodal & 13 & 0.000 & 7.7\% \\ \hline
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{100} & \textbf{0.217} & \textbf{48.0\%} \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:kappa}
\end{table}

Overall agreement is fair ($\kappa = 0.217$), and its structure is informative rather than merely disappointing. Item-level disagreement is dominated by \emph{tie-versus-commit style}: GPT-4o's swap-consistency rule produces near-universal ties on indistinguishable content (all Claude cells: judge1 tied 26/26), while the Claude judge commits to a side on the same transcripts (e.g., 10 wins for Dissonance vs.\ 2 for Multimodal on the Claude backbone) --- and conversely, the Claude judge defaults to tie on most GPT-4o mini cells where GPT-4o commits. At the directional level --- asking only whether the two judges lean the same way when both commit --- the judges agree in \textbf{6 of 6} cells with non-tie signal: both lean Baseline on Qwen-vs-Baseline (7--4 and 6--2), both lean Dissonance on Qwen-vs-Multimodal, both cells of DeepSeek, and GPT-4o mini-vs-Multimodal (the second judge there never selects Multimodal, 0/12). Agreement is highest exactly where the quality signal is strongest (Qwen/DeepSeek vs.\ Baseline: $\kappa = 0.50$--$0.60$) and degenerates where content is indistinguishable (Claude cells). This pattern --- reliable direction, unreliable ties --- is consistent with the judge-misalignment findings of Haydarov et al.\ \cite{haydarov-etal-2025-towards} and motivates the three-layer design: no single instrument (absolute scores, one judge's pairwise verdicts) carries the conclusions alone.

\section{Hypothesis Verdicts}

\textbf{H1 (Dissonance Advantage).} \emph{Partially supported --- on 2 of 4 backbones, with no backbone significantly against.} On GPT-4o mini, Dissonance-Aware achieved the highest score on all seven metrics (highest-or-tied-highest as pre-registered), significantly over Baseline on the three CTRS craft dimensions and over Multimodal on six of seven, with pairwise confirmation (net 0.680 and 0.800). On DeepSeek V3.2 it is tied-highest on the absolute scale and significantly preferred in pairwise judgment ($p = 0.00004$). On Qwen it is neutral against Baseline (all layers converge: $t = -1.90$ n.s., pairwise $p = 0.649$, both judges leaning Baseline) while still significantly ahead of Multimodal and Emotion. On Claude all conditions are indistinguishable at the judge's ceiling. The pre-registered ``highest or tied-highest'' criterion is met on GPT-4o mini and DeepSeek, unmeasurable on Claude, and not met against Baseline on Qwen.

\textbf{H2 (Backbone Robustness).} \emph{Partially supported, with a sharper robust core.} The advantage over Baseline is not architecture-independent: it is significant where scoring headroom exists and undetectable or neutral otherwise. What \emph{is} robust across backbones is the ordering against the other information conditions: Dissonance-Aware never loses significantly to any condition on any backbone, beats Multimodal significantly on two backbones (GPT-4o mini $t = 7.94$; Qwen $t = 2.53$) with the cross-family judge concurring directionally in every committed cell, and beats Emotion wherever Emotion is measurable. The dissonance \emph{computation and framing}, rather than the presence of vocal metadata, is the architecture-agnostic active ingredient.

\section{Computational Overhead}

Multimodal emotional reasoning incurs additional computational cost relative to text-only generation. To quantify this overhead, we measured per-component inference times across 100 dialogues generated under the Baseline and Dissonance-Aware conditions using Claude Sonnet 4.6 as a representative backbone.\footnote{Timings were measured on the identical pipeline architecture (same components: client LLM, vad-bert, Zonos TTS with Director, WavLM SER, librosa descriptors, therapist LLM). The client-memory update changes prompt lengths marginally and adds or removes no components, so the per-component overhead carries over directly.} Each dialogue consists of 10 turns (1,000 total utterances).

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\centering
\caption{Inference time breakdown: Baseline condition (Claude Sonnet 4.6, 100 dialogues, 1,000 utterances)}
\begin{tabular}{lrrc}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Count} & \textbf{Avg (s)} & \textbf{Total (s)} \\ \hline
Client LLM           & 1,000 & 3.58 & 3,581.31 \\
Discrete Text        & 1,000 & 0.00 & 0.02 \\
Therapist LLM        & 1,000 & 3.90 & 3,902.07 \\
VadBERT              & 1,000 & 0.05 & 54.60 \\ \hline
\multicolumn{3}{r}{\textbf{Total (all dialogues):}} & \textbf{7,538.00} \\
\multicolumn{3}{r}{\textbf{Avg per dialogue:}}   & \textbf{75.38} \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:inference-baseline}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\centering
\caption{Inference time breakdown: Dissonance-Aware condition (Claude Sonnet 4.6, 100 dialogues, 1,000 utterances)}
\begin{tabular}{lrrc}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Count} & \textbf{Avg (s)} & \textbf{Total (s)} \\ \hline
Client LLM           & 1,000 & 3.79 & 3,785.06 \\
Discrete Speech      & 1,000 & 0.00 & 0.02 \\
Discrete Text        & 1,000 & 0.00 & 0.02 \\
Dissonance Comp.     & 1,000 & 0.00 & 0.00 \\
Librosa              & 1,000 & 0.09 & 87.08 \\
Therapist LLM        & 1,000 & 5.54 & 5,543.96 \\
VadBERT              & 1,000 & 0.06 & 62.16 \\
WavLM                & 1,000 & 0.25 & 254.27 \\ \hline
\multicolumn{3}{r}{\textbf{Total (all dialogues):}} & \textbf{9,732.57} \\
\multicolumn{3}{r}{\textbf{Avg per dialogue:}}   & \textbf{97.33} \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:inference-dissonance}
\end{table}

The Dissonance-Aware pipeline adds \textbf{21.92 seconds per dialogue} (+29\%) relative to the Baseline, an increase from 75.38s to 97.33s. Three components account for the majority of this overhead:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Therapist LLM (+1.64s/response).} The single largest contributor. Dissonance-Aware prompts are richer: they include VA values, vocal descriptors, and the dissonance delta flagged for therapeutic exploration, which elicits longer, more deliberative therapist responses. The increased token generation is inherent to the deeper reflection the framework is designed to encourage.
    \item \textbf{WavLM speech emotion extraction (+0.25s/utterance).} Each client audio waveform is processed through a WavLM-based SER model to predict valence and arousal from the acoustic signal. This step is absent in the Baseline condition, where only vad-bert (text-side) runs.
    \item \textbf{Librosa vocal descriptor extraction (+0.09s/utterance).} Pitch, loudness, and speaking rate are computed from the raw audio using signal processing. While individually negligible, this step must run for every utterance.
\end{enumerate}

The Dissonance Computation itself is instantaneous ($< 0.01$s): it is a simple arithmetic comparison of scalar VA values. VadBERT runs in both conditions with near-identical timing.

\textbf{Cost--Benefit Tradeoff.} For GPT-4o mini, where the Dissonance framework yields significant CTRS gains (Guided Discovery $+0.24$, Focus $+0.34$, Strategy $+0.17$) and a pairwise net win-rate of 0.680 over Baseline while closing the Understanding gap to Claude's unaided baseline to 0.15 points, the 29\% time increase represents a favorable tradeoff: a modest computational premium for a measurable therapeutic quality gain. For Claude, where all conditions sit at the measurement ceiling, the overhead is harder to justify on quality grounds alone, though the framework's value there may lie less in measurable improvement than in the qualitative shift toward exploring hidden affect at moments of detected dissonance. The practical implication is that the Dissonance-Aware framework is most cost-effective when paired with smaller, lower-cost models that have the most room to benefit from additional emotional reasoning.
